<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cape Town</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1652">1652</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cape Town is a port city on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/table-bay">Table Bay</a> in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. It was founded in 1652 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-van-riebeeck">Jan van Riebeeck</a> as a supply station of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> and grew into the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and then British rule. Since 1910 it has been the legislative capital of South Africa.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cape-town.jpg" alt="Cape Town" width="320" height="185" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Der Berzerker from Washington, DC, USA · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Camps_bay_(53460319478)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Cape Town stands on the shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/table-bay">Table Bay</a>, at a latitude of about 33.55 degrees south. Table Mountain, with near vertical cliffs and a flat summit more than 1,000 metres high, forms a mountainous backdrop with Devil's Peak and Lion's Head on either side and creates the City Bowl, which holds the central business district. To the south the Cape Peninsula runs 40 kilometres into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a> and ends at Cape Point, and many suburbs lie on the Cape Flats, a plain that joins the peninsula to the mainland. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robben-island">Robben Island</a> in Table Bay is part of the city, and Table Mountain National Park lies within its boundaries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest known remains of human occupation in the region were found at Peers Cave in Fish Hoek in the late 1920s; earlier estimates of an upper Pleistocene origin have since been revised to the mid-Holocene. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolomeu-dias">Bartolomeu Dias</a>, the first European to reach the area, arrived in 1488 and named it the Cape of Storms. John II of Portugal renamed it the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a>, because the opening of a sea route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> raised such optimism, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a> recorded sighting it in 1497. In 1510, at the Battle of Salt River, the Portuguese admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-almeida">Francisco de Almeida</a> and sixty-four of his men were killed by the Khoikhoi Goringhaiqua, who used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> trained to respond to whistles and shouts.</p><p>Ships of several nations, mainly Portuguese, regularly stopped in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/table-bay">Table Bay</a> in the late sixteenth century on the way to the Indies. They traded tobacco, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and iron with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khoekhoe">Khoikhoi</a> clans of the region for fresh meat and other provisions. In 1652 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-van-riebeeck">Jan van Riebeeck</a> and other employees of the United East India Company were sent to establish a way-station for ships travelling to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>, and they built the Fort de Goede Hoop, later replaced by the Castle of Good Hope.</p><p>The settlement grew slowly because adequate labour was hard to find. The shortage led the local authorities to import enslaved people from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>, many of whom are ancestors of the present Cape Coloured and Cape Malay communities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Cape Town is the legislative capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, the seat of its Parliament, and the capital of the Western Cape. It is the country's oldest city. It has the highest density of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-dutch">Cape Dutch</a> buildings in the world, a style that combines the architectural traditions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. Mostert's Mill in Mowbray, built in 1796, is the only complete <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windmill">windmill</a> in South Africa. The Cape Floristic Region, including Kirstenbosch National Botanical Garden, was declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2004.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cape Town” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Harold Macmillan's “Wind of Change” speech in <b>Cape Town</b> in 1960 acknowledged the inevitability of African decolonisation, and the wave of independence that followed, beginning with Ghana in 1957 and Nigeria in 1960, ended Britain's African empire within little more than a decade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Cape Town</b> grew into a cosmopolitan port with Muslim, Christian and Jewish communities, and the South African College, founded in 1829, became the University of Cape Town.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cape-colony">Cape Colony · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The republic kept the Union’s territory, its three capitals of Pretoria, <b>Cape Town</b> and Bloemfontein, and its rule over South West Africa, which the United Nations later declared illegal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">Republic of South Africa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Then the gold of the Witwatersrand shifted the economic centre of South Africa from the Cape to the Transvaal, drew railways from <b>Cape Town</b>, Durban and Delagoa Bay to the Rand, and created Johannesburg, which became the country’s largest city.</blockquote><a href="/empire/south-african-republic">South African Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Union had three capitals: Pretoria for the government, <b>Cape Town</b> for parliament and Bloemfontein for the Appellate Division, a compromise South Africa still keeps.</blockquote><a href="/empire/union-of-south-africa">Union of South Africa · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Through early contact with the Philippines, words such as dalam hati, “sympathy”, luwalhati, “glory”, tengah hari, “midday”, and sedap, “delicious”, entered Tagalog and other Philippine languages, and the early settlement of a Cape Malay community in <b>Cape Town</b> brought many Classical Malay words into Afrikaans.</blockquote><a href="/language/malay">Malay · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
